"""
# Parser: an inline `match` value may be terminated by the enclosing list

**Category:** Syntax / Self-hosted frontend parity
**Bug:** doc/08_tracking/bug/parser_inline_match_terminated_by_list_2026-08-01.md

## Overview

A `match` used as a VALUE inside a call's argument list, a struct-literal field
list, an array literal or a dict literal ends at that list's separator or
closing bracket — the `,` / `)` / `]` / `}` shares the last arm's line, so the
lexer has not flushed a DEDENT yet.

The self-hosted arm loop in `parse_match_arms_common` only stopped on DEDENT or
EOF, so the terminator fell through to the caseless-arm branch and was handed to
`parse_expr` as if it began another pattern:

- `Box(a: match x: ... "other", b: x)` → `expected pattern, found Comma`
- `Box(b: x, a: match x: ... "other")` → `expected pattern, found RParen`

The Rust seed parses both. This blocked the hosted-WM showcase gate at
`src/os/hosted/hosted_web_content_session.spl:983` — a `semantic_target_id:`
field whose value is an inline `match` followed by the field-terminating comma.

A parse error means this file will not load, so the declarations below are
themselves the grammar coverage. The `it` blocks assert that each inline match
still selects the right arm, which is what a mis-terminated arm list would
corrupt.

## Syntax

```simple
Box(
    a: match x:
        1: "one"
        _: "other",      # <- field-terminating comma, not another pattern
    b: x
)
```

## Verification scope and provenance

This manual's scenarios are maintained in this source docstring because the
current human-documentation branch does not extract the compact `it(...)`
blocks below. They are correlated with all nine executable scenario titles
and all 23 assertion lines. This is manual-derived documentation, not proof
that the generator extracted those blocks correctly.

The two previously failing call-argument scenarios passed under the Phase1
bootstrap diagnostic runtime, including zero and negative inputs. Seven prior
passing scenarios were not rerun. This does not qualify the native frontend
or imply a new full-suite execution.

**Requirements:** doc/02_requirements/feature/sspec_documentization_maintenance.md (REQ-SSPEC-UNIT)
**Plan:** doc/03_plan/sspec_modernization_plan.md
**Design:** doc/05_design/infra/sspec/modern_sspec_typed_evidence_design.md
**Research:** doc/01_research/domain/sspec_documentization_maintenance.md

## Scenarios and exact expected results

### ends a struct-literal field at the field-terminating comma

Call the named grammar fixture with each listed input and compare its result
to the exact expected text. The original declarations remain in this file.

- `assert_equal(match_field_then_comma(1), "one")`
- `assert_equal(match_field_then_comma(7), "other")`

### ends the last struct-literal field at the closing paren

Call the named grammar fixture with each listed input and compare its result
to the exact expected text. The original declarations remain in this file.

- `assert_equal(match_field_then_rparen(1), "one")`
- `assert_equal(match_field_then_rparen(7), "other")`

### ends a call argument at the argument-separating comma

Call the named grammar fixture with each listed input and compare its result
to the exact expected text. The original declarations remain in this file.

- `assert_equal(match_call_arg_then_comma(1), "one/1")`
- `assert_equal(match_call_arg_then_comma(7), "other/7")`
- `assert_equal(match_call_arg_then_comma(0), "other/0")`
- `assert_equal(match_call_arg_then_comma(-7), "other/-7")`

### ends the last call argument at the closing paren on the arm's line

Call the named grammar fixture with each listed input and compare its result
to the exact expected text. The original declarations remain in this file.

- `assert_equal(match_last_call_arg_then_rparen(1), "one/1")`
- `assert_equal(match_last_call_arg_then_rparen(7), "other/7")`
- `assert_equal(match_last_call_arg_then_rparen(0), "other/0")`
- `assert_equal(match_last_call_arg_then_rparen(-7), "other/-7")`

### ends an array element at the element-separating comma

Call the named grammar fixture with each listed input and compare its result
to the exact expected text. The original declarations remain in this file.

- `assert_equal(match_array_element_then_comma(1), "one+tail")`
- `assert_equal(match_array_element_then_comma(7), "other+tail")`

### ends case-spelled arms at the same terminators

Call the named grammar fixture with each listed input and compare its result
to the exact expected text. The original declarations remain in this file.

- `assert_equal(match_case_arms_then_comma(1), "one")`
- `assert_equal(match_case_arms_then_comma(7), "other")`

### lets a nested match end without consuming the outer terminator

Call the named grammar fixture with each listed input and compare its result
to the exact expected text. The original declarations remain in this file.

- `assert_equal(nested_match_then_comma(1, 2), "one-two")`
- `assert_equal(nested_match_then_comma(1, 9), "one-other")`
- `assert_equal(nested_match_then_comma(7, 2), "other")`

### still ends a statement match at the DEDENT

Call the named grammar fixture with each listed input and compare its result
to the exact expected text. The original declarations remain in this file.

- `assert_equal(match_as_statement(1), "one")`
- `assert_equal(match_as_statement(7), "other")`

### still ends a local-bound match at the DEDENT

Call the named grammar fixture with each listed input and compare its result
to the exact expected text. The original declarations remain in this file.

- `assert_equal(match_bound_to_local(1), "one")`
- `assert_equal(match_bound_to_local(7), "other")`

"""

use std.spec.step

use std.spec

struct Box:
    a: text
    b: i64

fn take_two(a: text, b: i64) -> text:
    return "{a}/{b}"

# --- struct-literal field, comma-terminated (the hosted_web_content_session shape)

fn match_field_then_comma(x: i64) -> text:
    val v = Box(
        a: match x:
            1: "one"
            _: "other",
        b: x
    )
    return v.a

# --- struct-literal field, terminated by the closing paren -------------------

fn match_field_then_rparen(x: i64) -> text:
    val v = Box(
        b: x,
        a: match x:
            1: "one"
            _: "other"
    )
    return v.a

# --- call argument, comma-terminated -----------------------------------------

fn match_call_arg_then_comma(x: i64) -> text:
    return take_two(match x:
            1: "one"
            _: "other",
        x)

# --- last call argument, `)` on the last arm's own line ----------------------

fn take_flipped(b: i64, a: text) -> text:
    return "{a}/{b}"

fn match_last_call_arg_then_rparen(x: i64) -> text:
    return take_flipped(x, match x:
            1: "one"
            _: "other")

# --- array literal element, comma-terminated ---------------------------------

fn match_array_element_then_comma(x: i64) -> text:
    val items = [match x:
            1: "one"
            _: "other",
        "tail"]
    return items[0] + "+" + items[1]

# --- `case`-spelled arms take the same exit ----------------------------------

fn match_case_arms_then_comma(x: i64) -> text:
    val v = Box(
        a: match x:
            case 1: "one"
            case _: "other",
        b: x
    )
    return v.a

# --- a match nested inside an arm must not swallow the outer terminator ------

fn nested_match_then_comma(x: i64, y: i64) -> text:
    val v = Box(
        a: match x:
            1: match y:
                2: "one-two"
                _: "one-other"
            _: "other",
        b: x
    )
    return v.a

# --- regressions: the DEDENT-terminated forms must keep working --------------

fn match_as_statement(x: i64) -> text:
    var r = "unset"
    match x:
        1: r = "one"
        _: r = "other"
    return r

fn match_bound_to_local(x: i64) -> text:
    val r = match x:
        1: "one"
        _: "other"
    return r

describe("inline match terminated by the enclosing argument or field list"):
    it("ends a struct-literal field at the field-terminating comma"):
        # @req REQ-SSPEC-UNIT
        # @req REQ-SSPEC-UNIT
        assert_equal(match_field_then_comma(1), "one")
        assert_equal(match_field_then_comma(7), "other")

    it("ends the last struct-literal field at the closing paren"):
        assert_equal(match_field_then_rparen(1), "one")
        assert_equal(match_field_then_rparen(7), "other")

    it("ends a call argument at the argument-separating comma"):
        assert_equal(match_call_arg_then_comma(1), "one/1")
        assert_equal(match_call_arg_then_comma(7), "other/7")
        assert_equal(match_call_arg_then_comma(0), "other/0")
        assert_equal(match_call_arg_then_comma(-7), "other/-7")

    it("ends the last call argument at the closing paren on the arm's line"):
        assert_equal(match_last_call_arg_then_rparen(1), "one/1")
        assert_equal(match_last_call_arg_then_rparen(7), "other/7")
        assert_equal(match_last_call_arg_then_rparen(0), "other/0")
        assert_equal(match_last_call_arg_then_rparen(-7), "other/-7")

    it("ends an array element at the element-separating comma"):
        assert_equal(match_array_element_then_comma(1), "one+tail")
        assert_equal(match_array_element_then_comma(7), "other+tail")

    it("ends case-spelled arms at the same terminators"):
        assert_equal(match_case_arms_then_comma(1), "one")
        assert_equal(match_case_arms_then_comma(7), "other")

    it("lets a nested match end without consuming the outer terminator"):
        assert_equal(nested_match_then_comma(1, 2), "one-two")
        assert_equal(nested_match_then_comma(1, 9), "one-other")
        assert_equal(nested_match_then_comma(7, 2), "other")

    it("still ends a statement match at the DEDENT"):
        assert_equal(match_as_statement(1), "one")
        assert_equal(match_as_statement(7), "other")

    it("still ends a local-bound match at the DEDENT"):
        assert_equal(match_bound_to_local(1), "one")
        assert_equal(match_bound_to_local(7), "other")
